  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the steering wheel connect to?",
      "a": "The steering wheel is fixed to the steering column, a shaft that runs down to the pinion gear near the front wheels. Turning the wheel twists the column and the pinion.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the steering column?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the pinion gear in steering?",
      "a": "The pinion is a small round gear on the end of the steering column. Its teeth mesh with the rack, so when it turns it slides the rack sideways.",
      "asks": [
        "What does the pinion do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the steering rack?",
      "a": "The rack is a straight bar with teeth along it, running across the car. The pinion slides it left or right, and its ends push the tie rods that swivel the wheels.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a rack and pinion work in a car?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is power steering?",
      "a": "Power steering adds extra force to help you turn the wheels. Most new cars use an electric motor on the column or rack; older cars used a pump pushing oil into a cylinder on the rack.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is steering easy in modern cars?",
        "How does electric power steering work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do only the front wheels steer?",
      "a": "Steering the front wheels lets the car's nose lead it round a bend while the rear wheels follow, which is stable and easy to control. Some cars and lorries also steer the rear wheels a little to turn more tightly.",
      "asks": [
        "Do the back wheels of a car turn?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Most modern cars use rack and pinion steering.",
    "In a turn the inside front wheel is angled more sharply than the outside one because it follows a tighter circle; this is called Ackermann steering geometry.",
    "Electric power steering only uses energy while you are steering, so it saves fuel compared with an engine-driven oil pump."
  ],
